<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Windward Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Windward Islands are the southern islands of the Lesser Antilles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, including Dominica, Martinique, Saint Lucia, Saint Vincent and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenadines">Grenadines</a>, and Grenada. Long contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the British islands were grouped under a single governor from 1833, an arrangement that lasted until 1960, when most of them had joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> Federation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/windward-islands.jpg" alt="Windward Islands" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MaligneRange · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Indies_-_Island_Groups.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the time of Spanish contact the Kalinago were among the dominant peoples of the Caribbean, living throughout the <b>Windward Islands</b>, Dominica and the southern Leeward Islands, including Guadeloupe, and possibly Barbados.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Windward Islands</b> was an administrative union rather than a federation: each island kept its own laws, treasury and, at first, assembly, under a lieutenant governor or administrator.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-windward-islands">British Windward Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>Martinique came under direct royal rule in 1674 and became the seat of the governor-general of the French <b>Windward Islands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-martinique">Colony of Martinique</a></li><li><blockquote>The West Indies Federation came into being on 3 January 1958, joining ten British territories: Jamaica, Trinidad and Tobago, Barbados, the Leeward Islands of Antigua, Saint Kitts-Nevis-Anguilla and Montserrat, and the <b>Windward Islands</b> of Grenada, Saint Vincent, Saint Lucia and Dominica.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a></li></ul></section>
